    "lawCode": "Amd §§6-102, 6-104, 6-106 & 6-108, Energy L",
    "lawSection": "Energy Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3914\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the energy law, in relation to the composition,\nresponsibilities and discretion of the state energy planning board and\nmaking technical changes related thereto\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend the State energy planning law contained in\nArticle 6 of the Energy Law, which was added by chapter 433 of the\nlaws of2009, to streamline the operations of the State Energy\nPlanning Board (Board) and streamline the energy planning process to\nreduce costs and increase the Board's efficiency.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Energy Law § 6-102 to remove the\nPresident of the New York State Urban Development Corporation (UDC)\nand the Presiding Officer of the Bulk System Operator (BSO) as\nmembers of the Board. The BSO would serve in a consulting role.\nSection 1 also clarifies that each of the two Regional Planning\nCouncils would have an advisory role in the energy planning process\nthrough submission of a report to the Board.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend Energy Law § 6-104, which currently\nrequires forecasts of energy supply and demand for five, ten and\nfifteen years. The amendment would allow the Board to determine the\nappropriate forecast period. In addition, section 6-104 currently\nrequires forecasts for each region of the State as well as the State\nas a whole. The amendment would require regional analyses only if the\nBoard determines it to be feasible and necessary. Section 6-104 also\ncurrently provides that energy-related decisions of a State agency,\nboard, commission or authority shall be reasonably consistent with\nthe energy plan and any such decision which is not reasonably\nconsistent with the plan shall be deemed in compliance provided the\nagency, board, commission or authority makes a finding that the\nrelevant provisions of the plan \"are no longer reasonable or\nprobable.\" The amendment would replace the finding requirement with a\nprovision that no decision of an agency, board, commission or\nauthority shall be deemed by a court to be unreasonably inconsistent\nwith the plan unless the Board makes a determination to that effect.\nThis amendment is consistent with the prior Article 6 of the Energy\nLaw which sunset in 2003.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would amend Energy Law § 6-106 to make changes\nconforming to the changes in section 2 of the bill relating to energy\nforecasting periods. Section 3 would also allow the Board to require\na single comprehensive submission of the statutorily required\n\ninformation from industry or trade groups of utilities and major\nenergy suppliers, rather than individual submissions from their\nmembers.\n\nSection 4 of the bill would amend Energy Law § 6-106(6) to require the\nBoard to hold public comment hearings prior to adopting an amendment\nto the energy plan, but to make evidentiary hearings, if requested,\ndiscretionary, rather than mandatory.\n\nSection 5 of the bill would amend Energy Law § 6-108 to make certain\nadditional studies, including studies of the reliability of the\nelectric power system, discretionary rather than mandatory.\n\nSection 6 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nArticle 6 of the Energy Law was created by chapter 433 of the laws\nof 2009. It reenacts much of the prior Article 6 which sunset in 2003.\nChapter 433 established the new State Energy Planning Board, requires\nthe Board to adopt a new State Energy Plan by March 15, 2013, and\nestablished the process by which the new Energy Plan is to be\ndeveloped.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new proposal.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nChapter 433 of the Laws of 2009 established an independent energy\nplanning board, to create a comprehensive and permanent energy\nplanning process in New York, and thereby to promote energy\nindependence. This bill would maintain the central elements of\nChapter 433, while making certain technical changes that would\nstreamline the planning process, reduce the burden the planning\nprocess currently places on state agencies, and ensure that the Board\ncan focus its efforts on the most important aspects of the energy\nplanning process.\n\nFirst, it would streamline the Board, by removing the UDC President of\nthe New York and the Presiding Officer of the BSO. The UDC interests\non the Board will be represented by the Commissioner of Economic\nDevelopment, who is also a Board member. While input from the BSO\nwill be essential in developing future energy plans, the BSO is\nessentially a market participant, and given the Board's\nresponsibility to establish energy policy for the electricity and\nother energy sectors, including the BSO on the Board potentially\nraises conflict of interest issues.\nContinued BSO participation on the Board would permit one industry\nsector (electricity) to participate in Board deliberations, without\n\npermitting similar participation from other industry groups, such as\nthe natural gas or energy services industry sectors.\n\nSecond, the bill makes clear that the role of Regional Planning\nCouncils on the Board is advisory. While input from Regional Planning\nCouncils will help to provide focus on more granular level energy\nsystem needs, it would be difficult and unwieldy to add up to an\nadditional 18 non-voting members to the Board as current law\nprovides. To provide this participation at the optimal level, the\nRegional Planning Councils' are best suited as facilitators, to\nidentify and\ncoalesce regional needs, and present to the Board for consideration a\nreport and recommendations specific to their regions on a schedule to\nbe determined by the Board.\n\nThird, the bill would give the Board discretion to determine the\nforecast period for the Plan and to require comprehensive group\nsubmissions from electric and from utilities. This will eliminate the\npotential for unnecessary studies and effort. Finally, the bill would\nreturn to language in previous energy planning laws requiring the\nBoard to determine whether an agency action is inconsistent with the\nEnergy Plan. This amendment will provide adequate protections to\nensure that the Energy Plan policies are adequately carried out, but\nwill relieve State agencies from the requirement to produce\nunnecessary, time consuming and costly consistency analyses.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThe bill is expected to produce modest savings to NYSERDA and the\nagencies of the ex officio members.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThe bill would take effect immediately.\n\n",
