    "lawCode": "Amd §§6-102, 6-104 & 6-106, Energy L",
    "lawSection": "Energy Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3914A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the energy law, in relation to the composition,\nresponsibilities and discretion of the state energy planning board and\nmaking technical changes related thereto\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend the state energy planning law, as added by\nchapter 433 of the laws of 2009, to change the composition of the\nenergy planning board and streamline its operations.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Energy Law § 6-102 to remove the\nPresident of the New York State Urban Development Corporation, the\ndirector of the state emergency management office and the chair of\nthe consumer protection board and add the commissioner of the\ndivision of homeland security and the commissioner of agriculture and\nmarkets as members of the Board. Section 1 also clarifies that each\nof the two Regional Planning Councils would have an advisory role in\nthe energy planning process implemented through submission of a\nreport to the Board. Section 1 also adds the division of homeland\nsecurity to the list of agencies providing staff services to the Board.\n\nSection 2 would amend Energy Law § 6-104, which currently requires\nforecasts of energy supply and demand for five, ten and fifteen\nyears. The amendment would allow the Board to determine the\nappropriate forecast period. In addition, § 6-104 currently requires\nforecasts for each region of the state as well as the state as a\nwhole. The amendment would require regional analyses only if the\nBoard determines it to be feasible and necessary.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would amend Energy Law § 6-106 to require at\nleast three public hearings on the draft state energy plan in each of\nthe two regions defined in §6-102. Section 3 would also make changes\nconforming to the changes in section 2 of the bill relating to energy\nforecasting periods. Section 3 would also allow the Board to require\na single comprehensive submission of required information from\nindustry or trade groups of utilities and major energy suppliers,\nrather than individual submissions from their members.\n\nSection 4 of the bill would amend Energy Law §6-106(6) to require the\nBoard to hold public comment hearings prior to adopting an amendment\nto the energy plan, but to make evidentiary hearings, if requested,\ndiscretionary, rather than mandatory.\n\nSection 5 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nArticle 6 of the Energy Law was created by chapter 433 of the laws of\n2009. It reenacts much of the prior Article 6 which sunset in 2003.\n\n\nChapter 433 established the new State Energy Planning Board, requires\nthe Board to adopt a new State Energy Plan by March 15, 2013, and\nestablished the process by which the new Energy Plan is to be\ndeveloped.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new proposal.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nChapter 433 of the Laws of 2009 established an independent energy\nplanning board, to create a comprehensive and permanent energy\nplanning process in New York, and thereby to promote energy\nindependence. This bill would maintain the central elements of\nChapter 433, while making certain technical changes that would\nstreamline the planning process, reduce the burden the planning\nprocess currently places on state agencies, and ensure that the Board\ncan focus its efforts on the most important aspects of the energy\nplanning process.\n\nFirst, it would streamline the Board, by removing the President of the\nNew York UDC, the director of the state emergency management office\nand the chair of the consumer protection board and adding the\ncommissioner of the division of homeland security and the\ncommissioner of agriculture and markets. The UDC interests on the\nBoard will be represented by the Commissioner of Economic\nDevelopment, who is also a Board member. Participation by the\ncommissioner of agriculture and markets will be important to\nrepresent the interest of that important sector.\n\nSecond, the bill makes clear that the role of Regional Planning\nCouncils on the Board is advisory. While input from the Regional\nPlanning Councils will help to provide focus on more granular level\nenergy system needs, it would be difficult and unwieldy to add up to\nan additional 18 non-voting members to the Board as current law\nprovides. To provide this participation at the optimal level, the\nRegional Planning Councils are best suited as facilitators, to\nidentify and coalesce regional needs, and present to the Board for\nconsideration a report and recommendations specific to their regions\non a schedule to be determined by the Board.\n\nThird, the bill would give the Board discretion to determine the\nforecast period for the Plan and to require comprehensive group\nsubmissions from electric and from utilities. This will eliminate the\npotential for unnecessary studies and effort.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThe bill is expected to produce modest savings to NYSERDA and the\nagencies of the ex officio members.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThe bill would take effect immediately.\n\n\n",
